From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Fri, 23 Sep 1994 10:50:31 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-418] SCI: role for Q.M in neurobiology

Mike Price,
X-Message-Number: #94-9-397:

>These "quantum effects" you would only expect to operate
>under conditions of extreme coherency, like a few millionths of a degree
>above absolute zero.  Thermal noise is pretty effective destroyer of
>quantum interference effects.

This is the conventional dismissal for these ideas.  This response may be
motivated by the idea that what is being proposed is analogous to
superconductivity, which generally does require extremely low temperatures.
However, I have yet to see anyone directly argue against the suggestion of
Frolich that pumped phonon systems might exist at physiological
temperatures.  Michael Lockwood discusses these ideas at length in his book
"Mind, Brain, and the Quantum" on pp. 251-260.

Summarizing Lockwood's discussion, Frolich argues for the existence of
long-range coherent phenomena in biological tissues based on the occurrence
of widespread loosely coupled dipole oscillators (such as transmembrane
proteins in neural tissue).  Systems of phonons associated with the
longitudinal frequency modes of these coupled oscillators vibrate at
microwave frequencies.  The dipole oscillators themselves are continuously
pumped by active cellular processes, and prevented from falling into
equilibrium.  If the rate at which energy is supplied is high enough,
energy is stored in the lowest collective frequency mode of the phonon
system rather than being dispersed into the surrounding "heat bath".  New
phonons are created in this lowest collective frequency mode, the result
being a coherent state which is analogous to Bose-Einstein condensation.

Lockwood goes on to cite more recent authors as confirming Frolich's ideas
via several theoretical approaches (molecular Hamiltonian, etc.).  In
addition, there are currently unexplained phenomena that could be explained
by Frolich's ideas.  Lockwood cites two: the rate of growth of yeast cells
can be influenced by exposure to microwave radiation at sharply defined
resonance frequencies, and the "Raman effect", which is a peculiar kind of
laser light scattering by living cells.

I have summarized all this here because I would like to hear directed
criticisms of these basic ideas, which are specifically targeted at
producing a mechanism for high temperature quantum coherence phenomena in
biological tissues.

Doug

