From: Charles000@aol.com
Date: Fri, 23 Sep 94 14:39:34 EDT
Subject: [#94-9-420] copyright of the future

Nick Szabo had responded to the concept of embedded encoding
of materials for copyright purposes: 

>>  the design, each of our future "works" will get a
>>  "handle"--sort of like a fingerprint--unique to each item.  

>Nobody has ever designed such a "fingerprint" or "handle" that is 
>not easy to crack.  Even if such were possible it would not stop
>a cracker from stealing the work with the "finger" pointing
>at the victim rather than the cracker.

>Nick Szabo    szabo@netcom.com

My response is that, although my idea here would only apply to 
digital bit map inagery, and possibly sound files (I do a lot of work 
in the area of digital art media, and copyrighting all of my newly
raytraced images and animations is a constant hassle and expense,
as I am sure it is for everyone with this sort of application/
interest)

Have you had a look at Romana Machado's excellent encrypting
technique (as per her Stego software) for embedding numerical
data into the pixel data sets of a bit map image???  Maybe this is
a bit far fetched, but I like her ideas about being able to "mask"
hidden information "inside" a bit map file, to the extent that this
could be a stealth signature embedded directly into the image.

Obviously, this could not only be the original artists "signature"
data, but other "key" sets of relevant information as well, and 
this, of course, can be pre-encrypted via more traditional methods
before being embedded into the image data.  This does not 
necessarily prevent someone from pirating your image or sound
files, but it sure would be a defendable proof of origination to be
able to extract your embedded signature data from pirated 
material at a future time when it becomes relevant to do so.

Sadly, we live in a world where "defensive creativity" has become
a defacto fact of life.  

Sincerely
Charles

