Hi, all!

Greg Newton writes:
> Earlier on when I was receiving tape recorded 
> material from them - there was an artical mentioning the famed Alcor 
> Foundation, they were discussing that Biostasis would not work
> because, once a person was frozen then thawed a significant time
> later, they would lose their "sense of self" due to the large
> discontinuity.  They were sort of saying that 
> a persons' conciousness would not be preserved even though
> their body tissue would be restored - comments anyone?

Speaking as an individual who has done some investigation of cryonic 
preservation methods, I'd like to say that this is ridiculous.  Of course, 
my information may be out of date, given how quickly "current" 
information is changing these days.  To the best of my knowledge, no 
one yet knows what the exact mechanisms for storing memories, 
consciousness, or identity are.  That would make it difficult to make 
statements like "Biostasis will not work" because "a person's 
consciousness would not be preserved."  Of course, the same applies 
to the reverse of "Biostasis *will* work."  Right now, we know simply 
that biostasis might work.

If they have more detailed data on exactly how consciousness is 
stored, I would *very much* like to know.  And I mean *data*, not idle 
speculation or wishful thinking, please. :-)

--Tanya

